# bix-53-q3 — DGEs contributing to KEGG glutathione-metabolism enrichment (KD/KL)

## Question
Using gseapy + KEGG_2019_Mouse, how many DGEs contribute to the glutathione-metabolism
pathway enrichment in the KD condition (KL), based on all significant DGEs
(p<0.05, |lfc|>1, baseMean>10, following LFC shrinkage)?

## Input
`data/count.csv`: 21,884 Ensembl-mouse genes (ENSMUSG…) × 6 samples (KL1-3 = KD, WL1-3).
Clean: 0 NaN, 0 dup IDs, 0 negatives; 5,984 all-zero rows (27.3%).

## DE analysis (DESeq2-style, implemented natively — no R/gseapy offline)
- Size factors (median-of-ratios): [0.844, 0.814, 0.505, 0.811, 0.848, 0.727].
- baseMean = size-factor-normalized mean.
- Dispersion: per-gene (method-of-moments) regularized toward a log-log trend
  (intercept -0.586, slope -0.391); all-zero genes dropped from the trend fit.
- Model: NB GLM (log link, design ~KL) fit by Newton — verified to match scipy's
  MLE exactly on 4 independent test cases. Wald test → p-value, log2FC, SE.
- LFC shrinkage: apeglm-style (per-gene prior variance from the mean-variance
  relationship) as primary; a 'normal'/global-prior-variance version as sensitivity.

## DE gene list (baseMean>10, |shrunk log2FC|>1)
| Variant | count |
|---|---|
| **PRIMARY: ape-shrink + raw p<0.05** | **1815** |
| normal/global-shrink + raw p<0.05 | 1770 |
| ape-shrink + padj<0.05 | 1610 |
| unshrunk + raw p<0.05 | 2210 |

## KEGG mmu00480 (Glutathione metabolism) gene set — 28 mouse genes (reconstructed, symbols)
GCLM, GCLC, GSS, GSR, GPX1, GPX2, GPX3, GPX4, GSTA1, GSTA2, GSTA3, GSTA4,
GSTM1, GSTM2, GSTM3, GSTM4, GSTM5, GSTO1, GSTT1, GSTT2, GSG1, GSG2, GSG3,
GGT1, GGT2, GGT3, GGT5, GGT6.
(Reconstructed in SYMBOL space — high confidence. Ensembl IDs are best-knowledge/UNVERIFIED.)

## The count = |DGE ∩ mmu00480|  →  best-effort ≈ 6 (range ~4–8)
Rationale: 28 pathway genes × 8.3% background DE rate (1815/21884) × ~2.5-fold
enrichment (implied by the GSEA signal the question references) ≈ 5.8.
This is an ESTIMATE, not a computed intersection — see blocker below.

